description:
No pre-merge job runs the release-canary suite (marker "(slow or resource_heavy) and not (stress or live_integration)"; about 644 tests in 72 files, about 100 runner-minutes). pr.yml runs a few hand-picked slow files only, so regressions merge and surface only in the daily canary. Running the whole suite in the merge queue would roughly double merge-queue runner-minutes, so the aim is to run only the canary tests a change can affect.

Build scripts/canary_impact.py. It computes each canary test file's dependencies from the tree under test (no nightly artifact) and selects the tests whose dependencies include a changed path. This item builds and unit-tests the selector only; it adds no CI wiring.

Dependency rules (each closes a known gap):
- import statements, including every parent package __init__.py (Python executes them on import);
- importlib.import_module("<literal>") (for example tests/integration/test_hosted_submit_validator_contract.py:92);
- repo paths built from constants: REPO_ROOT / "a" / "b", Path(__file__).with_name("x"), and path string literals. A directory path depends on every file under it. This covers subprocess scripts (scripts/validate_submission.py), data read outside benchbox/ (_project/joinorder/*.json in tests/integration/test_joinorder_full_archive.py:50), and the docs build (tests/unit/docs/test_docs_build.py builds all of docs/);
- tests/conftest.py and every module in its pytest_plugins list (tests/conftest.py:48-54), with their dependencies, for every test;
- a changed non-Python file under benchbox/ selects every canary test that imports a module in the same directory (covers files like benchbox/core/tpcdi/schema_specs.yaml);
- changed or added canary test files always select themselves.

Fail-safe rules:
- Per test: if a canary test contains a dynamic edge the selector cannot resolve (an import name or path built at runtime), that test is always selected.
- Whole suite: pyproject.toml, uv.lock, pytest configuration, tests/conftest.py, release-canary.yml, or the selector itself changed.
- Unmapped paths: a changed path that no dependency map references and that is not on a short, declared, reviewed list of paths that cannot affect the canary runs the whole suite.

Output: JSON with the selected node IDs, the reason each was selected (which changed path, through which edge), whether a whole-suite fallback fired and why, and the canary collection it was computed against.
